WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.020
How does the operating system

2
00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:03.820
protect itself from your program?

3
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.640
Hardware distinguishes ordinary application execution

4
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.780
from privileged kernel execution. Without

5
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.800
that distinction, a badly behaved

6
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.700
application could rewrite the very

7
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.300
rules meant to contain it.

8
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.700
The course calls these user

9
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.940
mode and monitor mode, also

10
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.020
called kernel or supervisor mode.

11
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.260
Its mode-bit convention is one

12
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.620
for user mode and zero

13
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.320
for monitor mode. Treat those

14
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.840
numbers as the course convention

15
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.700
rather than a universal encoding

16
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:32.880
for every processor.

17
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.600
A privileged instruction is permitted

18
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.320
only in the privileged mode.

19
00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.460
For example, loading protection registers

20
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.780
must not be available to

21
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.060
arbitrary user code. Otherwise an

22
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.840
application could enlarge its own

23
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.600
permitted memory region and defeat

24
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:48.140
the checks.

25
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.180
When a controlled trap or

26
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.360
interrupt enters the kernel, hardware

27
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.860
provides the required privileged transition.

28
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.360
Returning to user execution restores

29
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.300
the appropriate less-privileged state. A

30
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.840
user program does not simply

31
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:02.860
assign a value to the

32
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.040
mode bit and crown itself

33
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.000
ruler of the machine.

34
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:07.340
Root or administrator is an

35
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.660
operating-system account privilege. Kernel mode

36
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.320
is a hardware execution mode,

37
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.580
so they are not the

38
00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.760
same thing. A program launched

39
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.040
by an administrator still executes

40
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.040
ordinary instructions in user mode

41
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.440
and requests protected services through

42
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.420
the operating system.

43
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.800
Now consider the tutorial memory

44
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.220
example. The base register is

45
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.380
hexadecimal one thousand, and the

46
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.380
limit register is also hexadecimal

47
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.280
one thousand, meaning a region

48
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.180
of that size. For an

49
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.100
absolute physical address, the legal

50
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.320
interval starts at the base

51
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.020
and stops just before base

52
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:41.680
plus limit.

53
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.680
Adding the base and size

54
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.100
gives hexadecimal two thousand as

55
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.700
the exclusive upper boundary. Therefore

56
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.260
the last legal address is

57
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.460
hexadecimal one F F F.

58
00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.740
Check it against both inequalities:

59
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.480
at least the base, and

60
00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:57.860
strictly less than the upper

61
00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:58.340
boundary.

62
00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:00.620
An access to hexadecimal two

63
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.140
thousand fails the strict upper-bound

64
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.200
check and traps. Do not

65
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.820
mistake a limit expressed as

66
00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:07.980
a size for an inclusive

67
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.740
final address. Later, execution-time relocation

68
00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.180
will check a logical offset

69
00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.640
against the size before adding

70
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.160
the base; that is a

71
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.400
different address convention.

72
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.280
The slides also classify hardware

73
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.460
input-output instructions as privileged. An

74
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.900
application requests input-output through operating-system

75
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.960
services, which can check validity

76
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.060
and permission. A request to

77
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.220
open a file is not

78
00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.540
permission to issue arbitrary device-control

79
00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:35.140
instructions.

80
00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.020
Things to take note of.

81
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.280
Keep account privileges separate from

82
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.100
processor mode, and keep a

83
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.840
region size separate from its

84
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.560
exclusive upper boundary. The tutorial

85
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.060
address one F F F

86
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.300
is legal in this example;

87
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.160
two thousand is the first

88
00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:51.400
address outside it.
